/**

@page page_exahype_compute_graph Lowering into a compute graph

\tableofcontents

@ref page_exahype_numerical_scheme "adding a numerical scheme" describes how a
scheme populates a peano4.api.taskgraph.Graph with Task instances and
dependencies. That graph is still a purely descriptive, implementation-level
model: it says which @ref glossary_observer "algorithmic steps" exist, which mesh entity each works on
(Task.GridEntity.VERTEX/FACE/CELL) and which steps have to happen
before which others - it does not yet say anything about Peano @ref glossary_events_and_action_sets "action sets",
observers or mesh traversals. Turning the graph into those is the job of a
*task graph compiler*, peano4.api.taskgraph.compiler.


# The task graph compiler contract

A task graph compiler is a function

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
def translate(graph: peano4.api.taskgraph.Graph, identifier: str) -> List[peano4.api.solversteps.Step]:
  ...
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

that takes a Graph plus an identifier for the surrounding algorithmic phase
(e.g. "SetupSimulation", "TimeStep", "Plot") and returns a list of
peano4.api.solversteps.Step objects. In Peano terminology, a Step corresponds
to an @ref glossary_observer "Observer": for every mesh traversal, the traversal automaton is handed an
observer that distributes the grid @ref glossary_events_and_action_sets "events" among the @ref glossary_events_and_action_sets "action sets" tied to it.
This is exactly the second generator layer the
@ref page_exahype_design_philosophy "design philosophy" page describes:
individual solver steps get mapped onto action sets and traversals here.

peano4.api.taskgraph.compiler.Sequential.translate() is the baseline
compiler shipped today. It linearises the task graph into one @ref glossary_observer "Observer" per
task, in an order that respects every dependency, and prefixes every
generated Observer's name with `identifier` so that observers coming from
different phases never clash even if their tasks happen to share a name.
Being sequential, it throws away any parallelism the graph might encode -
it is deliberately the "no fusion, no reordering" baseline that smarter
compilers (which might, for instance, fuse independent tasks into one
traversal) can be measured against.


# Where this fits in code generation

A numerical scheme's construct_output() only ever builds a Graph; nothing
about Peano's C++ classes is visible to it. Only once
generate_Peano4_project() (@see exahype2.api.Project) runs the graph through
a compiler does the description turn into actual Step/Observer objects that
the rest of the Peano 4 Python API can lower into generated C++, exactly
mirroring how exahype2.api.SolverToCPPFiles turns a Solver's descriptive PDE
terms into generated methods.

While developing or debugging a numerical scheme, peano4.api.taskgraph.Graph.visualise()
is often the fastest way to check that a task graph has the intended shape
before it ever reaches a compiler.

*/
